2. Important correction on “business,” not “enterprise”

I will treat this as business AI governance.

That means the governance should focus on business use cases, business users, business workflows, business value, and business risk.

It should not assume a huge enterprise AI bureaucracy.

It should be practical enough for:

* merchandising;
* supply chain;
* customer service;
* call center;
* club support;
* field communications;
* HR/L&D-adjacent training;
* finance/business analysis;
* technology pipeline evaluation;
* offshore capability center opportunities;
* AI Champions;
* business-led pilots;
* sanctioned business AI tools;
* Co-work / Connectors-style connected capabilities.

The important nuance from the tool research is this:

Business-tier AI tools may require more explicit governance because default settings and connected-app behavior can vary by plan.

For example, OpenAI’s documentation says that in ChatGPT Business, workspace admins can control enabled apps and role-scoped app permissions, and OpenAI’s release notes state that connectors are enabled by default for Business workspaces while admins/owners can disable specific connectors. OpenAI also states that, by default, it does not train on inputs or outputs from business users, including ChatGPT Team, ChatGPT Enterprise, and API.  

Microsoft 365 Copilot uses Microsoft Graph to access user data in the user’s context, including emails, chats, and documents the user has permission to access. Microsoft also says Copilot respects identity and permissions, inherits sensitivity labels, applies retention policies, supports audit of interactions, and follows admin settings. Microsoft’s admin guidance also covers governance of agents and connectors through lifecycle, deployment, DLP, and approval controls.  

Anthropic’s Claude connector guidance says connectors let Claude access apps and services, retrieve data, and take actions in connected services, and that Claude inherits each person’s permissions from the connected service. For Google Workspace connectors, Anthropic says Team and Enterprise plan owners must enable connectors before users authenticate, and Claude can only access the Gmail, Calendar, and Drive data for the connected Google account.  

For BJ’s, the business implication is clear:

Connectors and Co-work-style capabilities should be treated as a higher governance category than ordinary prompting because they can retrieve business content, summarize internal information, expose permissioning issues, or take action across connected systems.

That does not mean “block them.” It means govern them differently.

