Pillar 2: Cost and KPI measurement process

This maps to:

“Cost and KPI measurement process: measurement parameters, tracking process & ownership, pilot scaling criteria.”

Research-backed design principle

AI value should be measured at the workflow level first.

McKinsey’s 2025 AI research found that most organizations are still early in scaling AI and capturing enterprise-level value, while high performers are more likely to redesign workflows, embed AI into business processes, define human validation processes, and track KPIs.  

For BJ’s, that means the KPI system should not begin with “how many people used AI.”

Usage is not value.

A better measurement hierarchy is:

1. Access — Who has the tool?
2. Activation — Who has tried it?
3. Adoption — Who uses it repeatedly?
4. Workflow integration — Where is it embedded into real work?
5. Outcome improvement — What changed?
6. Business value — What financial, operational, member, or team member value resulted?
7. Risk control — Was value achieved without unacceptable risk?

Recommended KPI categories

1. Productivity metrics

Use these when AI helps people complete work faster.

Examples:

* time to draft communication;
* time to summarize document;
* time to prepare report;
* time to analyze feedback;
* time to build first version of training content;
* time to generate meeting summary;
* time to create presentation outline;
* time to complete research synthesis.

Measurement method:

* baseline time before AI;
* AI-assisted time;
* review/editing time;
* net time saved;
* number of users;
* frequency of task;
* percentage of time saved converted into higher-value work.

Important: do not count gross time saved without subtracting review time.

2. Quality metrics

Use these when AI improves consistency or quality.

Examples:

* quality audit score;
* manager approval rate;
* SME correction rate;
* number of factual errors;
* number of policy errors;
* tone/brand compliance score;
* completeness score;
* readability score;
* first-pass acceptance rate.

For generative AI, this is critical because speed without quality can create rework or risk.

3. Cycle-time metrics

Use these when AI reduces process duration.

Examples:

* time from request to first draft;
* time from intake to decision;
* time from issue identification to resolution;
* time from vendor input to summary;
* time from meeting to action plan;
* time from incident to triage.

4. Cost metrics

