Pillar 3: Risk and appropriate-use governance

This maps to:

“Risk & appropriate use governance: drafting and aligning a public-facing AI governance and principles for member/public use; ongoing education / approach to Co-work and Connectors; process to tackle, escalate and resolve any appropriate use breaches.”

Research-backed design principle

AI risk governance needs to be tiered.

Not every AI use case needs the same review.

A team member using AI to draft a meeting agenda is not the same as a business team using connected AI to summarize member complaints, produce customer service responses, or recommend operational decisions.

The BJ’s model should classify use cases into risk tiers.

⸻

Recommended BJ’s Business AI Risk Tier Model

Tier 0: Personal productivity, low sensitivity

Examples:

* brainstorming;
* rewriting non-sensitive text;
* drafting meeting agendas;
* summarizing public information;
* creating generic training examples;
* generating Excel formulas using dummy data;
* improving grammar.

Governance approach:

* allowed with standard guidance;
* no special intake required;
* education through AI Hub and training;
* no confidential/member/team member data unless approved tool and policy allow.

Tier 1: Internal business productivity with low-to-moderate sensitivity

Examples:

* summarizing internal meeting notes;
* drafting internal communications;
* creating project plans;
* analyzing non-sensitive internal process notes;
* building training drafts;
* creating stakeholder updates.

Governance approach:

* allowed in sanctioned business tools;
* user review required;
* follow data classification rules;
* publish reusable guidance/templates;
* may require manager awareness if used repeatedly in a process.

Tier 2: Business workflow support with measurable operational impact

Examples:

* recurring report generation;
* supply chain exception summaries;
* merchandising vendor analysis;
* call center knowledge article drafting;
* club operations FAQ generation;
* communications planning;
* policy search over approved internal content;
* AI-assisted training content production.

Governance approach:

* intake required;
* KPI definition required;
* business owner review;
* risk screening;
* pilot charter;
* human validation model;
* scale decision based on evidence.

Tier 3: Sensitive business, member, employee, financial, legal, or connected-data use

Examples:

* member complaint analysis;
* customer service response suggestions;
* employee-related content;
* pricing/promotion recommendations;
* vendor negotiations;
* financial forecasts;
* legal/policy interpretation;
* AI connected to internal repositories;
* Co-work / Connectors use;
* confidential strategy documents;
* automated workflow actions.

Governance approach:

* formal intake required;
* Security/Privacy/Legal/Technology review as applicable;
* data access review;
* human-in-the-loop required;
* accuracy testing;
* approval before pilot;
* monitoring after pilot.

Tier 4: High-risk or prohibited unless specially approved

Examples:

* AI making employment decisions;
* AI making member eligibility decisions;
* AI independently approving refunds/credits/financial outcomes;
* AI generating legal advice for action without review;
* AI using sensitive personal data without approval;
* AI monitoring employee behavior or emotions;
* AI making safety-critical decisions;
* AI generating deceptive content;
* AI impersonating BJ’s leaders or members;
* AI bypassing access controls;
* AI using unauthorized external tools with confidential data.

Governance approach:

* default stop;
* executive/legal/security/privacy review;
* AI Council decision if strategically important;
* documented exception process;
* no scaling without controls.

